comctl32: the tree expander triangle sits on cleared ground, so the tree lines stop at it.

Neutron draws the tree expander as a filled triangle (the …n-comctl32-treeview-triangle-expander patch; the boxed
+/- it replaced is what comctl32 draws once the visual style is off). The boxed glyph was a filled rectangle, so
the tree lines stopped at its edge; the bare triangle let them run straight through it. At an installer's row
height (FL Studio 2026's NSIS components tree, lab 2026-11-03) the lines crossing every triangle read as clutter.
His pick (C175) of three variants shown side by side: keep the triangle, clear the lines under it.

The square the glyph occupies is now filled with the item background before the triangle is drawn. The file
dialog's folder tree (TVS_EX_NEUTRON_SHELL chevrons) returns before this code and is unchanged.

Lab, 178 + this (lab 178f), FL Studio installer's Choose Components page: the vertical line breaks around each
triangle (zoomed capture); on 178 it runs through them. A test tree (buttons, lines, root lines, check boxes)
the same.


--- a/dlls/comctl32/treeview.c
+++ b/dlls/comctl32/treeview.c
@@ -2437,6 +2437,14 @@
         HBRUSH old_br = SelectObject(hdc, br);
         HPEN np = CreatePen(PS_SOLID, 0, GETTXTCOLOR(infoPtr->clrText));
         HPEN op = SelectObject(hdc, np);
+        /* NEUTRON 2026-11-03: the glyph sits on cleared ground. The boxed +/- it replaced was a filled
+         * rectangle, so the tree lines stopped at its edge; the bare triangle let them run straight through
+         * it, and at an installer's row height (FL Studio's components tree) that read as clutter. */
+        RECT ground = { centerx - s - 1, centery - s - 1, centerx + s + 2, centery + s + 2 };
+        HBRUSH bk = CreateSolidBrush(clrBk);
+
+        FillRect(hdc, &ground, bk);
+        DeleteObject(bk);
 
         if (item->state & TVIS_EXPANDED && !(item->state & TVIS_EXPANDPARTIAL))
         {
